by nicolas-grekas at 2025-10-20T08:00:22Z

@wouterj thanks for the high-level defense of the fluent PHP format. However, I think you didn’t realize strongly enough that the arguments I put in the PR description are cold facts about it. There’s nothing to discuss when talking about the rigidity of the resulting config description. Before-normalizers are just hard walls that make the format a dead end: we cannot envision any forward path for that format that’d fix that.

Since you linked work by @TomasVotruba: he’s been a big proponent of the fluent PHP format, even calling YAML deprecated on X. While that’s certainly not true, he put his time where his mouth is and created those migration tools. He then also wrote about the issues with the format, as highlighted by @GromNaN. I interpret this push as a wish to move to a PHP-based format - not  specifically for the fluent one we have today.

If I may, I’d sum up the situation as follows: there’s a strong desire, at least from some, to have a PHP-based config format. We do want to address this desire. The fluent PHP one we have today has been our tentative solution so far. But now we know: you enter this format with bells and whistles and end up in a PR to hell. The devil is in the details (pun intended, but still in line with my stance 😉). I invite anyone that wants to get a taste of that to try to make the examples in https://github.com/symfony/symfony-docs/pull/21499 work.

About the timing of submission: we lost two months on the PR deprecating XML - two months of silence. I take responsibility for that. We have only one life and planning to continue dealing with the fluent format for five more years would be a huge distraction - three years of 7.4LTS is already too much. I'd also feel irresponsible for not actively preventing new code from entering this dead-end format.

I'm therefore going to take leadership and responsibility on this topic, and merge this PR. I also have approvals from core-team members who worked deep on the topic, so I'm confident that's the right path forward.

Let's keep our energy and goodwill to improve the array-shape format. We can start by the issues you mentioned.
